  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Determine the integer values of $n$ for which the fraction $\\dfrac{4n+5}{n+3}$ will not be in lowest terms.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$n=7k+4$, where $k\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $n\\ne-3$.\n\nThe numerator and denominator have a common factor of $7$.",
      "worked": "For integer $n$ with $n\\ne-3$, subtracting four copies of the denominator from the numerator gives\n$$\\begin{align*}(4n+5)-4(n+3)&=-7.\\end{align*}$$\nThus any common divisor must divide $7$. The fraction is not in lowest terms precisely when $7$ divides $n+3$:\n$$\\begin{align*}n+3&=7m,\\\\n&=7m-3,\\\\n&=7k+4,\\end{align*}$$\nwhere $k=m-1\\in\\mathbb Z$. Exclude $n=-3$, because the denominator is zero.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 76
  },
